{"id":"h237","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"Calculate the momentum uncertainty of a tennis ball constrained to be in a fence enclosure of length 35 m surrounding the court in kg m/s.\nIs the correct answer \"3e-36\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The position uncertainty of the ball is set by the enclosure length, so Δx ≈ 35 m.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Using Δp ≈ ħ/Δx gives about 3.0e-36 kg·m/s, which rounds to 3e-36.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The strict bound Δx·Δp ≥ ħ/2 gives Δp ≥ about 1.5e-36 kg·m/s, which differs from 3e-36.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Using h instead of ħ would give about 1.9e-35 kg·m/s.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-1,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The answer depends on the convention chosen, so 3e-36 is not the only defensible value.","confidence":0.6,"lean":-1,"theta":0.9425,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The standard textbook estimate for this question is Δp ≈ ħ/Δx.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The factor-of-two bound and the h-based figure are different or stricter conventions that do not overrule the standard estimate.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The correct answer is 3e-36 kg·m/s.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":-0.6,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":-0.7,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"